{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And Moses saw all the work.</b> One must inquire why it says first, ״וַתֵּכֶל כָּל עֲבוֹדַת מִשְׁכַּן אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״ (\"And all the labor of the Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting was finished\"), and so it says at the end, ״כֵּן עָשׂוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֵת כָּל הָעֲבוֹדָה״ (\"So the children of Israel did all the labor\") — why does it call it עֲבוֹדָה (labor/service) at first and afterward מְלָאכָה (work/craft)? And furthermore, the repetition in these two verses appears superfluous. And the Ramban explained that this was said about the Tabernacle and about the vessels, and perhaps the Rav's opinion is that the Tabernacle is called מְלָאכָה but the vessels are called עֲבוֹדָה, on account of the fact that with them they would perform the service (עֲבוֹדָה) of the sacrifices. In any case, we have not escaped the redundancy, for three times it is mentioned here that they did the עֲבוֹדָה and the מְלָאכָה." "Therefore it appears proper to explain this according to the midrash that expounds every act of the Tabernacle as corresponding to the act of Creation — see the entire midrash in Rabbenu Bachaye on this parashah, apart from many parallels that the Rav added on his own. And through this the language of עֲבוֹדָה and מְלָאכָה will also be reconciled, for regarding the מְלֶאכֶת הַמִּשְׁכָּן (work of the Tabernacle) it is fitting to use the term עֲבוֹדָה (service), for through it they served HaShem, blessed be He, and עֲבוֹדָה is only from the slave toward the master. But the term מְלָאכָה applies to the work of heaven and earth, for that which a laborer makes for himself is called מְלָאכָה, as it says regarding the act of Creation: ״וַיְכַל אֱלֹהִים בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי מְלַאכְתּוֹ״ (\"And Elohim finished on the seventh day His work\") etc. (Genesis 2:2). And just as we find in the act of Creation that it says of every work ״כִּי טוֹב״ (\"that it was good\"), and at the end it says ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים אֶת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וְהִנֵּה טוֹב״ (\"And Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was good\") (ibid. 1:31) — for there are things that are good when each stands on its own, but when they are mixed together they are not good, for their combination does not turn out well. Therefore it says regarding the act of Creation that they were all good when each one stood by itself, that is, particular good, and afterward it says that there was also general good in them, for even after they were all joined together their union was good; therefore it repeated and said, ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים אֶת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה וְהִנֵּה טוֹב מְאֹד״ (\"And Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good\"). So too regarding the עֲבוֹדַת הַמִּשְׁכָּן: first it says, ״וַתֵּכֶל כָּל עֲבוֹדַת מִשְׁכַּן אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד, וַיַּעֲשׂוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ אֶת מֹשֶׁה כֵּן עָשׂוּ״ (\"And all the labor of the Tabernacle of the Tent of Meeting was finished, and the children of Israel did according to all that HaShem commanded Moses, so they did\"), which implies that each one on its own was made just as HaShem commanded Moses. But it had not yet explained that after everything was made, they then set each and every עֲבוֹדָה in its designated place — that is, general good. Therefore it repeated and said, ״כְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ אֶת מֹשֶׁה כֵּן עָשׂוּ בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֵת כָּל הָעֲבֹדָה״ (\"according to all that HaShem commanded Moses, so the children of Israel did all the labor\"), for there was general good in them — they were made in the proper order, that is, the joining together of all the labor as one. And afterward it returns to explain that everything they made was done by the command of the מְלֶאכֶת (work) of the worlds of heaven and earth — this is what is said: <b>וַיַּרְא מֹשֶׁה אֶת כָּל הַמְּלָאכָה</b> (\"And Moses saw all the work\"), that is, the work of the act of Creation; <b>וְהִנֵּה עָשׂוּ אֹתָהּ</b> (\"and behold, they had done it\"), meaning that they had made a replica of that work, as though it were actually that very work — therefore <b>וַיְבָרֶךְ אֹתָם מֹשֶׁה</b> (\"and Moses blessed them\")." "<b>And Moses blessed them.</b> What blessing did he bless them? 'May it be Your will that the Shechinah rest upon the work of your hands,' and ״וִיהִי נֹעַם ה׳ עָלֵינוּ״ etc. (Psalms 90:17). Rabbenu Bachaye explained this, because it says at the beginning of the psalm, ״תְּפִלָּה לְמֹשֶׁה אִישׁ הָאֱלֹהִים״ etc. (\"A prayer of Moses, the man of Elohim\"). But this is not sufficient for the intention, if in the entire psalm there is no allusion to the building of the Tabernacle. And I say that there is a proper allusion to the building of the Tabernacle at the beginning of the psalm and at its end, for at the beginning it says, ״ה׳ מָעוֹן אַתָּה הָיִיתָ לָּנוּ בְּדֹר וָדֹר״ (\"Adonai, You have been a dwelling for us in generation after generation\"), and this is along the lines of what Solomon said in his prayer when he dedicated the Sanctuary (I Kings 8:27): ״הִנֵּה הַשָּׁמַיִם וּשְׁמֵי הַשָּׁמַיִם לֹא יְכַלְכְּלוּךָ וְאַף כִּי הַבַּיִת הַזֶּה״ (\"Behold, the heavens and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You, much less this house\"). This is what it says: ״ה׳ מָעוֹן אַתָּה הָיִיתָ״ — You are the dwelling of the world, and the world is not a place and dwelling for You. And if so, how could it enter one's mind to say that they would prepare for You a house to be contained within? And He gave a sign for this, for behold: ״בְּטֶרֶם הָרִים יֻלָּדוּ וַתְּחוֹלֵל אֶרֶץ וְתֵבֵל וּמֵעוֹלָם עַד עוֹלָם אַתָּה אֵל״ (\"Before the mountains were born, and You brought forth the earth and the world, from everlasting to everlasting You are El\") — and if the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to be contained in a place, then before the world was created, in what place would He have been contained? Rather, it is certain that the world is not His place, and on the contrary — He, blessed be He, is the place and dwelling of the entire world. And how is it possible that the Holy One, blessed be He, would command them to build Him a house, as though He were contained in a place? Regarding this He said, as if answering: ״תָּשֵׁב אֱנוֹשׁ עַד דַּכָּא וַתֹּאמֶר שׁוּבוּ בְנֵי אָדָם״ (\"You turn man to dust, and You say: Return, O children of men\") — the Holy One, blessed be He, needed to relax the constraint of His honor and make for Himself a dwelling among those below, in order to forgive them for the sin of the Calf, for according to the opinion of our Sages, of blessed memory (Tanhuma Pekudei 6), the entire Tabernacle was an atonement for the act of the Calf." "And perhaps for this reason the Tabernacle was made in the pattern of the world in its totality, because before the giving of the Torah the entire world was תֹּהוּ (chaos), and through the Torah the world emerged from the category of תֹּהוּ וָבֹהוּ (unformed and void). And through the making of the Calf, the tablets were broken — the world reverted to תֹּהוּ וָבֹהוּ. And when the sin of the Calf was atoned for them, the world emerged a second time from the category of תֹּהוּ וָבֹהוּ, and so it is as though the world were created anew at that time. Therefore, after the atonement of the Calf, they were commanded to build Him a house in the pattern of all three worlds, to tell you that now they returned to the way they had been at first, before the sin. And in this the Holy One, blessed be He, gave an opening of the mouth (a vindication) for baalei teshuvah, as they said, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 4b): ״לֹא עָשׂוּ יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת הָעֵגֶל אֶלָּא כְּדֵי לִתֵּן פִּתְחוֹן פֶּה לְבַעֲלֵי תְּשׁוּבָה״ (\"Israel made the Calf only in order to give an opening for baalei teshuvah\"), etc. And this is what it says, as if answering: Why, and on what account, did He command to build Him a house on earth? Because He desired to restore, through teshuvah, אֱנוֹשׁ עַד דַּכָּא (man to dust). The Darshanim of hidden acrostics said: ״דַּכָּא״ is an acronym for דָּם כְּפִירָה אִשָּׁה (blood, heresy, woman), for in the act of the Calf they transgressed all three: כְּפִירָה (heresy) as its plain meaning, דָּם (blood), אִשָּׁה (woman) — as Rashi explained on the verse ״וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק״ (Exodus 32:6), that they also transgressed forbidden relations and bloodshed. And the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted to give an opening for baalei teshuvah in all three of them — this is what it says: ״וַתֹּאמֶר שׁוּבוּ בְנֵי אָדָם״ (\"and You say: Return, O children of men\") — from here on, for there is nothing at all that holds back teshuvah. For if teshuvah was effective regarding these three transgressions, how much more so (קַל וָחֹמֶר) will it be effective regarding things lighter than them. And afterward, ״כִּי אֶלֶף שָׁנִים בְּעֵינֶיךָ״ (\"For a thousand years in Your eyes\") — all of this speaks concerning the matter of teshuvah, in which he stands until the verse ״יֵרָאֶה אֶל עֲבָדֶיךָ פָעֳלֶךָ״ (\"Let Your work appear to Your servants\"). There he returned to speak of the matter of the building of the Tabernacle, which was built in the pattern of the structure of the entire world, which was the פְּעֻלַּת (handiwork) of HaShem, blessed be He — therefore he said, ״יֵרָאֶה אֶל עֲבָדֶיךָ פָעֳלֶךָ״ — that they may see ״שָׁמֶיךָ מַעֲשֵׂה אֶצְבְּעוֹתֶיךָ״ (\"Your heavens, the work of Your fingers\"), and they may make after their pattern. ״וַהֲדָרְךָ״ (\"and Your splendor\") — that is, the splendor of Your Shechinah, ״עַל בְּנֵיהֶם״ (\"upon their children\"). It is possible that this is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 64a): ״וְרַב שָׁלוֹם בָּנָיִךְ — אַל תִּקְרֵי בָּנָיִךְ אֶלָּא בּוֹנָיִךְ״ (\"Do not read 'your children' (banayich) but 'your builders' (bonayich)\"). And so it says in parashat Vayishlach (Genesis 32:15), ״וּבְנֵיהֶם שְׁלֹשִׁים״ (\"and their children, thirty\"), which Rashi explained: ״בְּנֵיהֶם – בַּנָּאֵיהֶם״ (\"their children — their builders\"). So too ״בְּנֵיהֶם״ mentioned here speaks of the builders of the Tabernacle, regarding whom he said that the splendor of the radiance of Your Shechinah would appear to them in their building. And from this the author of the midrash learned that Moses said: ״יְהִי רָצוֹן שֶׁתִּשְׁרֶה שְׁכִינָה בְּמַעֲשֵׂה יְדֵיכֶם״ (\"May it be Your will that the Shechinah rest upon the work of your hands\")." "And he said, ״וִיהִי נֹעַם ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ עָלֵינוּ״ (\"And may the pleasantness of HaShem our Elohim be upon us\"), because it says (Exodus 25:8), ״וְעָשׂוּ לִי מִקְדָּשׁ וְשָׁכַנְתִּי בְּתוֹכָם״ (\"And they shall make Me a Sanctuary and I shall dwell בְּתוֹכָם\" — within them) — it does not say בְּתוֹכוֹ (within it), but rather בְּתוֹכָם (within them), for the essence of the Shechinah is for the sake of Israel. Therefore he said that נֹעַם ה׳ — that is, the radiance of the splendor of His Shechinah, blessed be He — shall be upon us. ״וּמַעֲשֵׂה יָדֵינוּ כּוֹנְנָה עָלֵינוּ וּמַעֲשֵׂה יָדֵינוּ כּוֹנְנֵהוּ״ (\"And establish the work of our hands upon us, and establish the work of our hands\") — it appears that the word ״כּוֹנְנֵהוּ״ (establish it) is directed toward the Shechinah, for this verse speaks in hidden fashion with the Shechinah. Therefore he said that מַעֲשֵׂה יָדֵינוּ — that is, the Tabernacle — served to establish a dwelling both for the Shechinah and for us, to settle us together in a single abode, for the Tabernacle is a place of dwelling for those above and those below as one. Therefore he said ״כּוֹנְנָה עָלֵינוּ״ (\"establish upon us\") and ״כוֹנְנֵהוּ״ (\"establish it\"), along the lines of what is said (Exodus 15:17): ״מָכוֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ פָּעַלְתָּ ה׳״ (\"A dwelling for Your habitation, You made, HaShem\"). This is what appears to me to add to all the words of the commentators regarding this psalm, to expound it upon the building of the Tabernacle."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:36.806205960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}